iMac 5K: recover tile link loss after the completed display commit

Follow-up to 5k-slave-link-preserve-lock.patch. The earlier 2026-09-08 test
boot reported initial lock but remained stretched, with slave AUX returning
EIO. Its HPD guard was active. A full DPMS restart restored both links and
the owner confirmed a normal desktop.

Move deferred work after the complete atomic commit. For a tiled modeset,
observe both active tile links eight times (250 ms initially, then 500 ms).
Treat AUX errors, incomplete lane lock, and absent sink reception as failure.
Use DC's normal blank/disable/enable link-loss recovery, bringing the slave
up after the root if the root also failed. At most two recoveries per
modeset; reserve the final observation for verification. Healthy links are
not restarted. Re-sync after recovery, then verify on a later work pass.

Serialize against DC programming; do not revive DPMS-off streams or act
during suspend/reset/shutdown. Stop requeueing before teardown cancellation.
This repairs the observed failure after it occurs; the underlying cause of
the post-enable loss is not yet established. No compositor startup reset is
installed.

Validated on hardware 2026-09-08 (first boot off this build came up at a
correct 5120x2880 with no VT or DPMS cycle) and promoted to the default
install stacks. The going-down hunk anchors only on context the verbose
stack and the lean pair share, so it applies on top of either. Tile
identification uses the stitch layer's amdgpu_dm_link_is_tiled_stitch_*
helpers rather than a panel-patch helper, whose name differs between the
two stacks.

Every line of the sequence carries one tag, so a single
`journalctl -k -b 0 | grep 'APPLE5K: link-health'` reads the whole story of a
boot: the build banner at init (before any panel is detected, so a missing
banner means the wrong module booted), the arming, each numbered pass with
both links' DPCD status, and a final PASS or FAIL. The paths that silently
disarmed the checks -- no DC state, no active tiled pair, a pass skipped for
suspend or shutdown -- now say so rather than leaving the log to end after
the arming line.

